{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>עקב</b> Three times. One: 'he has magnified against me עקב.' And: 'he shall bruise עקב.' This is what they said of blessed memory concerning the serpent: woe for the great luminary that was lost from the world — this is 'also the man of my peace,' etc. 'he has magnified against me עקב.' And further they said concerning the blessing of the tribes: even though he compared Judah to a lion and Naphtali to a hind, he turned and blessed them all in one blessing, as written: 'each man according to his blessing he blessed them' — to say that he equated them all to the strength of a lion and to the lightness of a hind. And this is 'he shall bruise עקב': even though he compared Dan to a serpent, he included also Gad in that blessing."], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The three occurrences of עקב are cited from Job, Genesis (this verse), and Psalms respectively; the first quotation 'he has magnified against me עקב' is from Job 19:11 and the 'man of my peace' phrase from Psalms 41:10, both unaddressed in the original. The citation 'he shall bruise עקב' refers to this verse, Genesis 3:15. The saying about the serpent and the lost luminary appears in b. Chullin 139b."}, :source-witnesses [["Kitzur Baal HaTurim" "http://sefaria.org"] ["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:15:37.867221023Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}